1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,565
Every enterprise has a system nobody dares to touch.

2
00:00:03,565 --> 00:00:04,830
A mainframe ledger.

3
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:08,095
A warehouse batch file from 1994.

4
00:00:08,095 --> 00:00:10,027
A scan of a printed manifest.

5
00:00:10,027 --> 00:00:13,892
The data inside is quietly rotting, and no one can see it.

6
00:00:13,892 --> 00:00:16,090
NecroLegacy performs the autopsy.

7
00:00:17,796 --> 00:00:22,578
Drop in a legacy dump, a COBOL copybook, or a photo of a printed table.

8
00:00:22,578 --> 00:00:27,562
Three synthetic presets are built in, so you can try it with no real data.

9
00:00:38,987 --> 00:00:41,009
Five agents run in a fixed order.

10
00:00:41,009 --> 00:00:45,911
The schema archaeologist infers entities, keys and relationships from raw bytes.

11
00:00:45,911 --> 00:00:51,855
Deterministic checks and an isolation forest score the rot before any language model is involved.

12
00:00:51,855 --> 00:00:55,225
The model only explains what the numbers already found.

13
00:01:03,555 --> 00:01:06,238
The verdict arrives like a coroner's notice.

14
00:01:06,238 --> 00:01:09,958
Orphaned foreign keys and negative stock are always critical.

15
00:01:09,958 --> 00:01:13,983
Healthy specimens glow steadily, and the rotten ones glow crimson.

16
00:01:13,983 --> 00:01:21,179
Drag to orbit, open any specimen for its anomalies, and every interaction has a keyboard and screen reader equivalent.

17
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:26,251
Search the records in plain words, and the broken ones surface.

18
00:01:26,251 --> 00:01:33,203
Then the last agent resurrects the system: a typed FastAPI wrapper, syntax checked and ready to download.

19
00:01:34,105 --> 00:01:35,452
It reads paper too.

20
00:01:35,452 --> 00:01:40,343
Drop in a faded dot-matrix manifest, and the first pass runs locally.

21
00:02:10,555 --> 00:02:19,869
In the Vision X-Ray, confident regions are green, while smudged, torn or faded cells are crimson, so nothing unreadable is passed off as certain.

22
00:02:20,770 --> 00:02:23,002
NecroLegacy was built with Kiro.

23
00:02:23,002 --> 00:02:36,046
A nineteen requirement spec drove the design and the task list, steering files kept every agent on the same rules, and an MCP server lets Kiro inspect a legacy schema and query its rot.

24
00:02:36,046 --> 00:02:39,533
Live today on Cloud Run, Render, Neon and Upstash.

25
00:02:39,533 --> 00:02:40,370
NecroLegacy.

26
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:41,556
Autopsy the past.

27
00:02:41,556 --> 00:02:42,672
Ship the future.
